# Commander instance: openai/gpt-oss-120b, native MXFP4, port 8000.
#
# This file is the single source of truth for the commander's share of the Spark's
# unified memory. config/config.toml deliberately does not repeat it.

# Apply changes to this file with:  make restart-commander
#   NOT a single-service restart. The commander's 62 GB checkpoint cannot load while the
#   grunt holds ~28 GiB of the 121 GB unified pool -- the loader gets pages reclaimed as
#   fast as it allocates them and never converges, with no crash and no error to show for
#   it. So this target cycles the whole stack, commander first. Budget ~10 minutes and do
#   not interrupt it: a killed load leaves the pool contended and the next attempt is
#   slower than the last.

COMMANDER_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-120b
# Must match [models.commander].model in config/config.toml. health_check.py asserts it.
COMMANDER_SERVED_NAME=gpt-oss-120b

# MEASURED, not estimated. First boot at 0.55 failed with "No available memory for the
# cache blocks", and the profiler printed why:
#
#   vLLM sees ~119.6 GiB of device memory (not the nominal 128 GB)
#   model loading took          66.14 GiB   <- NOT the 60.77 GiB on disk; MXFP4 scales
#                                              and padding cost ~5 GiB on load
#   estimated CUDA graph memory  1.60 GiB
#   available KV cache memory   -1.95 GiB   <- i.e. 0.55 was ~2 GiB short of viable
#
# So the floor for this model is ~0.57 just to start, and anything useful needs more.
# 0.64 gives ~76.5 GiB, leaving ~8.8 GiB of KV cache after weights and CUDA graphs.
#
# Watch the "Available KV cache memory" and "maximum concurrency" lines on every boot;
# they are the ground truth for this number, and they move when the model, the context
# length, or the vLLM version changes.
#
# The default if you omit this flag is 0.92, which NVIDIA's release notes name as the
# cause of OOM on unified-memory systems. Explicit fractions are the documented fix, not
# a preference. Raise this only by taking memory from the grunt instance, and re-check
# `free -g` with both models loaded.
COMMANDER_GPU_FRACTION=0.64
# The KV cache must hold at least one full-length sequence, so this trades directly
# against concurrency. Measured, both from real boots:
#
#   16384 -> 10.01 GiB KV   (2026-08-19)
#   24384 ->  5.02 GiB KV   (2026-08-20, current)
#
# Half the KV for 1.5x the context. That still clears max-num-seqs 4, and it is worth
# having: synthesis at 16384 ran to 15,372 of 16,384 tokens -- 94% full -- which is why
# the sample of cited log lines sent to the commander is so tightly budgeted. More context
# means more evidence it can actually audit.
#
# Do not push much further without checking. At this rate ~32k would leave roughly 2 GiB,
# which is where "No available memory for the cache blocks" starts. Watch "Available KV
# cache memory" and "maximum concurrency" on every boot; if KV comes back negative the
# engine fails fast and 16384 is a one-line revert.
COMMANDER_MAX_MODEL_LEN=24384
# Small: the commander issues a handful of large, sequential calls, not a stream of
# concurrent ones.
COMMANDER_MAX_NUM_SEQS=4

# vLLM 0.24.0 exposes this as a nested JSON config arg; the dotted form below is
# equivalent to --structured-outputs-config '{"backend": "xgrammar"}'. Default is
# 'auto'. Other fields on the same config, should you need them:
#   .disable_any_whitespace        tighter JSON, fewer wasted tokens
#   .disable_additional_properties
#   .enable_in_reasoning           structured output inside reasoning-model output
COMMANDER_STRUCTURED_OUTPUT_BACKEND=xgrammar

# --- gpt-oss MXFP4 on GB10/SM121 -----------------------------------------------------
# Verified in nvcr.io/nvidia/vllm:26.07-py3 (vLLM 0.24.0) on this box:
#   * torch reports compute capability (12, 1) -- GB10 is sm_121, served by the sm_120
#     family binaries and compute_120 PTX that this image ships.
#   * the registered quantization methods include BOTH `mxfp4` and `gpt_oss_mxfp4`, so
#     gpt-oss has a dedicated path here. Quantization is auto-detected from the model
#     config; do not pass --quantization unless you are overriding that.
#
# This is NVIDIA's own DGX Spark image, so the upstream Marlin fallback bug
# (vllm-project/vllm#37030, which produced `"content": null` on the community
# vllm/vllm-openai images) is not the expected failure mode. `make health` still proves
# it rather than assuming it -- a guided-JSON round trip is cheap and the symptom is
# otherwise invisible until mid-investigation.
#
# If something does go wrong, these are the flags that EXIST in 0.24.0 (the older
# --mxfp4-backend / --mxfp4-layers / --moe-backend recipes floating around online are
# from earlier builds and will be rejected here):
#
#   --quantization mxfp4              force the generic path
#   --quantization-config <json>      per-layer-kind specs (linear, moe) + ignore patterns
#   --kv-cache-dtype fp8              buys KV headroom if 0.55 turns out too tight
#   --attention-backend <name>        override backend selection
#   --async-scheduling                throughput, irrelevant here, listed for completeness
#
# Do NOT set --reasoning-parser without testing. In 0.24.0 it lives under
# StructuredOutputsConfig, and a mismatched parser routes the entire response into the
# reasoning channel, leaving `content` empty -- which looks identical to a kernel
# problem from the client's side. This is now the likelier of the two empty-content
# causes on this image.
#
# Last resort is a config swap, not a code change: serve a known-good model here (see
# the commented [models.commander] block in config/config.toml) and keep going. The
# commander's contract is a JSON schema; the model behind it is interchangeable.
COMMANDER_EXTRA_ARGS=

# --- Alternative commander: Qwen3.8-27B -- TRIED AND REVERTED 2026-08-25 -------------
# Kept because the measurement is worth more than the config. Both variants were run to
# completion against cases/dns-tunnel and graded with scripts/grade.py:
#
#                        ground truth   coercion   malformed cites   tok/s   wall clock
#   gpt-oss-120b MXFP4      7/7 best       2-6/run      7-22          --      5.6 min
#   Qwen3.8-27B BF16        6/7            0            0            4.3     59.3 min
#   Qwen3.8-27B INT4        6/7            0            0           11.4     47.0 min
#
# Qwen is the better-behaved analyst by some distance -- it never once needed the
# tool-call coercion in coercion.py, never wrote a malformed citation, and wrote cleaner
# regexes. It is also 8x the wall clock, and the reason is structural rather than tunable:
#
#   this box is MEMORY-BANDWIDTH BOUND, at ~273 GB/s.
#
# A DENSE model reads every weight for every token. 55.6 GB / 273 GB/s = 204 ms/token,
# a 4.9 tok/s ceiling -- 4.3 observed. gpt-oss-120b is larger on disk but MoE, so only a
# fraction of its experts are read per token, which is why the 120B model is six times
# faster than the 27B one here. Quantizing to 4-bit did exactly what the arithmetic said
# (2.7x, and no measurable quality cost) and still lost, because 21 GB/token is still far
# more traffic than a sparse forward pass.
#
# The lesson generalises: on this hardware, choose commanders by bytes-moved-per-token,
# not parameter count. A dense model has to be very small before it competes.
#
# What did NOT limit Qwen: guided-decoding conformance, which was flawless. If a sparse
# Qwen3.5 MoE checkpoint appears, it is worth a second look -- uncomment below, and see
# `make weights-qwen38` / `make weights-qwen38-int4`.
# To trial it, comment out COMMANDER_MODEL/SERVED_NAME/GPU_FRACTION/MAX_MODEL_LEN above
# and uncomment this block, then swap the matching [models.commander] block in
# config/config.toml. Apply with `make restart-commander` (full ordered cycle).
#
# MEASURED on this box, 2026-08-24, with the BF16 checkpoint (Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B, 55.6 GB):
#
#   59.3 min for a 10-step investigation, 356 s/step, 4.3 tokens/s.
#
# That is bandwidth, not a bad setting. A DENSE 27B at BF16 reads all 55.6 GB of weights
# for every token: 55.6 / 273 GB/s = 204 ms/token, a 4.9 tok/s ceiling, against 4.3
# observed. gpt-oss-120b is bigger on disk but MoE, so only a fraction of its experts are
# read per token -- which is why the 120B model was SIX TIMES faster here. On this box the
# question is never how many parameters a model has, it is how many bytes move per token.
#
# Hence the 4-bit build below: 21.0 GB/token -> ~13 tok/s ceiling, ~3x the BF16 rate.
# `make weights-qwen38-int4`. The BF16 repo is still fetched by `make weights-qwen38` if
# you want to compare quantization damage against the 6/7 that BF16 scored.
#
# KV, from the repo's config.json (64 layers, 4 KV heads, head_dim 256): 256 KiB/token at
# BF16, 128 KiB at FP8. 131072 tokens costs 16 GiB of KV at fp8. With 21 GB of weights
# that is ~38 GiB all-in, hence the much lower fraction -- this frees most of the pool.
#
# As always: read "Available KV cache memory" and "maximum concurrency" on the first boot
# and correct from those, not from this comment.
#
# COMMANDER_MODEL=cyankiwi/Qwen3.8-27B-AWQ-INT4
# COMMANDER_SERVED_NAME=qwen3.8-27b
#
# 55.6 GB of BF16 weights load to roughly 55 GiB (MXFP4 padding does not apply here; this
# checkpoint is not quantized). 0.62 of 121 GB gives ~70 GiB, leaving ~13 GiB after
# weights and CUDA graphs -- enough for 131072 tokens ONLY with fp8 KV, which is why it is
# not optional below.
# COMMANDER_GPU_FRACTION=0.45
#
# 131072, not 262144: the native context would need 32 GiB of KV even at fp8, and 64 GiB
# at bf16. 131072 is 5x the gpt-oss setting and the largest that leaves room to breathe.
# If the loader reports negative KV, step down to 65536 (8 GiB fp8) -- a one-line revert.
# COMMANDER_MAX_MODEL_LEN=131072
#
# Required, not tuning: at 256 KiB/token bf16 this model cannot hold a full-length
# sequence in the available cache. Halving it is what makes 131072 reachable at all.
# COMMANDER_EXTRA_ARGS=--kv-cache-dtype fp8
#
# This is a multimodal checkpoint (Qwen3_5ForConditionalGeneration, image-text-to-text).
# We send text only. If vLLM allocates multimodal profiling memory that we never use, add
# --limit-mm-per-prompt '{"image": 0, "video": 0}' to COMMANDER_EXTRA_ARGS to reclaim it.
# Verify the flag exists in 0.24.0 before relying on it; `make health` is the arbiter.
